      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oven to 200C. In a large bowl, combine the olive oil, lemon juice, crushed garlic, oregano, thyme and a generous amount of black pepper. Add the potato wedges and toss to coat well."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Tip the potatoes into the rectangular baking tin and spread them into an even layer. Nestle the chicken thighs on top, skin-side up. Tuck the lemon slices and any remaining garlic cloves around the chicken."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour 100ml of water into the bottom of the tin. This helps the potatoes cook through and keeps the dish from drying out before the chicken is done."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Roast for 45 minutes, then check the potatoes. If the liquid has fully evaporated, add another splash of water. Continue roasting for 10 more minutes until the chicken skin is deep golden and the potatoes are tender."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Rest for 5 minutes before serving straight from the tin with the roasting juices spooned over."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "problem": "The potatoes are still firm when the chicken is done",
              "solution": "Cut them into smaller pieces next time or par-boil for 5 minutes before roasting."
            },
            {
              "problem": "The skin is not crispy",
              "solution": "Make sure the chicken goes in skin-side up with nothing on top of it. Pat the skin dry before seasoning."
            },
            {
              "problem": "The dish tastes bland",
              "solution": "Season generously before roasting and add more lemon juice at the table. Greek cooking leans heavily on salt, pepper and citrus."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Roast lemon chicken is a Sunday staple across Greece, cooked in wood-fired ovens in villages and in domestic ovens in cities alike. The lemon-egg technique known as "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "avgolemono-sauce"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "avgolemono"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " is sometimes stirred into the roasting juices at the end to create a silky sauce. The one-tin nature of this dish means the washing-up is minimal. It reheats well the next day with a little extra water to loosen the juices."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}